            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "I just looked at your 'MiniDYM' plugin. The workflow seem to be to check for every new buffer if it is present or not, and suggest \"Did you mean ...\" in latter case.\r\n\r\nPersonally I (and probably quite decent percentage of people) quite rarely open files with `edit` from command line. Usually it is from `telescope.nvim`, sometimes - from `nvim-tree`.\r\n\r\nIf it only to be checked on Neovim start, it's better to look at `VimEnter` event instead of `BufNewFile`.",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">I just looked at your 'MiniDYM' plugin. The workflow seem to be to check for every new buffer if it is present or not, and suggest \"Did you mean ...\" in latter case.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Personally I (and probably quite decent percentage of people) quite rarely open files with <code class=\"notranslate\">edit</code> from command line. Usually it is from <code class=\"notranslate\">telescope.nvim</code>, sometimes - from <code class=\"notranslate\">nvim-tree</code>.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">If it only to be checked on Neovim start, it's better to look at <code class=\"notranslate\">VimEnter</code> event instead of <code class=\"notranslate\">BufNewFile</code>.</p>",
            "bodyText": "I just looked at your 'MiniDYM' plugin. The workflow seem to be to check for every new buffer if it is present or not, and suggest \"Did you mean ...\" in latter case.\nPersonally I (and probably quite decent percentage of people) quite rarely open files with edit from command line. Usually it is from telescope.nvim, sometimes - from nvim-tree.\nIf it only to be checked on Neovim start, it's better to look at VimEnter event instead of BufNewFile.",
